\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Why a flight above the window costs a round trip, for the server's certificate flight of the card-authorization example (chain depth~3, 100\,Mbit/s, 30\,ms\xspace  RTT, MTU of 1\,500\,bytes, initial window of 10 segments). The vertical lines are the two endpoints, with time running downwards; each solid arrow is a burst of segments in flight, which takes half an RTT, and the dashed arrow is the acknowledgments coming back. (a) With ECDSA-P256 the flight has 3 segments and arrives after half an RTT. (b) With ML-DSA-65 it has 16. Ten segments leave at once, as one band, because they are only 0.12\,ms\xspace  apart; the sender then waits one RTT for the acknowledgments that open the window, and the six remaining segments leave. The times follow the ACK-clock recurrence of \cref  {eq:flight}; the model's total for this flight is 45.8\,ms\xspace  .}}{17}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ladder}{{4}{17}{Why a flight above the window costs a round trip, for the server's certificate flight of the card-authorization example (chain depth~3, 100\,Mbit/s, 30\ms RTT, MTU of 1\,500\,bytes, initial window of 10 segments). The vertical lines are the two endpoints, with time running downwards; each solid arrow is a burst of segments in flight, which takes half an RTT, and the dashed arrow is the acknowledgments coming back. (a) With ECDSA-P256 the flight has 3 segments and arrives after half an RTT. (b) With ML-DSA-65 it has 16. Ten segments leave at once, as one band, because they are only 0.12\ms apart; the sender then waits one RTT for the acknowledgments that open the window, and the six remaining segments leave. The times follow the ACK-clock recurrence of \cref {eq:flight}; the model's total for this flight is 45.8\ms }{figure.caption.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:ladder@cref}{{[figure][4][]4}{[1][16][]17}{}{}{}}
